# VEYLIX — line endings are a correctness issue, not a preference.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and deployed on Linux. Windows
# `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites a checked-out shell script to CRLF, and the
# resulting file fails on the server with:
#
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That failure looks like a missing interpreter and is actually a stray \r at
# the end of the shebang — so the rule is enforced here rather than diagnosed
# on a customer's host. `* text=auto eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf for the
# whole tree; the per-type rules below exist so the intent is readable.
*                 text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts, services and anything else executed on the server: LF, always.
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.bash            text eol=lf
*.service         text eol=lf
Caddyfile         text eol=lf
*.env.example     text eol=lf

# Source: normalise to LF in the repository, let each platform check out native.
*.go              text
*.ts              text
*.tsx             text
*.js              text
*.mjs             text
*.json            text
*.md              text
*.css              text
*.yml             text
*.yaml            text
*.sql             text

# Binary artifacts must never be line-ending converted.
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.ico             binary
*.woff            binary
*.woff2           binary
*.gz              binary
